Basic Information
Generic programing-> Multi type, compiler generates a version when use. I.e. Instantiation. 

  template<typename T> v.s. template<class T>
    Are otherwise equivalent...
      Compiler treams them interchangeably in this context->
      
    Unless...

      <class> and <typename> can not be used interchangeably in the same instance. (Dependent types) 
      For example->
        template <typename T>
        void foo(){
          class::some_type x; } 
      Does not work. 
      
      <typename>
        Created to reflect the general nature of templates. i.e. not JUST classes. 
        Recomened standard (Google)
        More ambiguity in the sense that <typename> does not denote its data-structure. (Or less ambiguity, depending on your perspective!) 
        C++ does not automatically assume <typename> represents a "type." 
        Dependent types-> (Types whose definition depends on a value. i.e. the value of a dependent type is not known until compile-time.
                          (Typically computed at compile time.
                          (Different than polymorphism-> 
                            Similar in the sense that both dependant types and polymorphism require auxilary information.
                            Can be used together. 
                            Dependent types (compile-time)-> what types mean at compile time. i.e. What type? 
                                                             Can have any* type but same behavior.
                            Polymorphism (run-time)->        allowing different types use a common interface. What use-case? 
                                                             Can have any* behavior but same type. 
      
  
      <class>
        Predates <typename>
        template<class T> must not always declare "T" as a type... it can be an int, function, etc. 
        Can restrict what a template parameter is-> declaring a "class template" means the template container is a class, instead of ____ .
          i.e. C++ can have template-classes within a template. WHICH can influence member access (private, public, and protected-> encapsulation)
        Has alternative meanings outside of this context.
        Operates at a different level of C++ type system->
                                                            ("class" declares a "class" type. 
                                                            ("class" defines the container type "class" 
      

