1 ~~~~~  Basic template example: 
    template <typename T>
    T add(T a, T b){ return a + b; }
    int c = add(2, 3);
    double y = add(2.5, 3.5);                       

      //the template declairs a template of type "T". I.e., instead of "int T," it's "general-programing-type T"
      //It's "type function (function-type value, function-type value) instead of (for example) int add( int a, int b).
      //That way you can pass and return what ever type (so long as its type-logic is compatible with this function). 

2 ~~~~~~  Explicit and deuction template argument example:
    template <typename T>
    T multiply(T a, T b){ return a * b; }
    auto x = multiply<int>(2,3);
    auto y = multiply<double>(2,3);
      //Both deduction and explicit instantiantion occurs in this example
      //Explicit Instantiation-> 
        (<int>(2,3) && <double>(2,3)
      //Deduction-> 
        ( C++17 compiler automatically deduces the type of arguments from the construtor arguments. 
        ( Reminder: <double>(2,3) converts the arguments to double. 
        ( The "auto" keyword automatically deduces the type at compile time
        ( So... type "auto"(the compiler will deduce its type as type int/double) x = the result of template function "T" 
            with the arugments (int 2, int 3). 
      // Note, we did not define the type of "x & y", the compiler 'deduced' the type of "x & y"-> redundant, but worth stating 'explicitly' ;)

3 ~~~~~  Two *type parameter with return-type deductionexample:
    template <typename T, typename U>
    auto add(T x, U y){ return x + y; }
      auto result = add(10, 2.5);                     result-> 12.5 (double) 
      
      //By creating two separate type "T" variables, the template can accept multiple types as arguments. 
      //So -> "10" and "2.5" can retain their intitial types (int and double) until the compiler deduces their new type 
          after the function "add" returns "x + y."

4 ~~~~~  Generic comparison example:
    template <typename T>
    bool greater(T a, T b){ return a > b; }
    greater(10, 5);
    greater(3.14, 2.71);
    greater('z', 'a');

    //Note, function 'greater' is not of type 'T', it is type 'bool'
    //Note, templates do not *require inheritance or a common base class. Templates can be global variables... consider best-practices though. 
    //function 'greater' only requires that type 'T' must be compatible with the '>' operator. 
  
